1. PURPOSE
The Billing, Subscription & Entitlement Architecture establishes the commercial-control system governing OneDraft plans, subscriptions, usage, quotas, metering, entitlements and organization-level commercial boundaries.
The purpose of this subsystem is to provide a deterministic relationship between commercial configuration and the capabilities available to an organization and its projects.
Billing shall remain separate from core project data, computational correctness, regulatory validation and architectural authority.
A commercial condition shall never alter the authoritative Project Model or invalidate previously generated project records.
The billing architecture shall therefore provide a controlled commercial layer over the OneDraft application while preserving project integrity, data ownership, security and lifecycle continuity.
2. ARCHITECTURAL ROLE
The Billing, Subscription & Entitlement subsystem forms the commercial authorization layer of OneDraft.
It determines which platform capabilities, usage quantities and service resources an organization is entitled to consume according to its active commercial configuration.
Primary responsibility: Manage subscriptions, plans, usage, quotas, metering and feature entitlements.
Primary inputs: Organization identity, subscription configuration, plan definitions, usage events, billing events, administrative configuration and entitlement requests.
Primary outputs: Entitlement decisions, quota states, usage records, billing records, subscription states and commercial events.
Authority: Commercial configuration and subscription state shall be authoritative for entitlement decisions.
Separation: Billing state shall not become authoritative for Project Model, compliance, validation or document correctness.
3. COMMERCIAL ARCHITECTURE
OneDraft shall separate commercial configuration into distinct concepts.
Plan: Defines a commercial product offering.
Subscription: Represents an organization's active commercial relationship with a plan.
Entitlement: Defines a capability or resource available under a subscription.
Quota: Defines a permitted quantity or usage limit.
Meter: Measures consumption.
Usage Record: Records measured consumption.
Billing Period: Defines the period over which usage and subscription activity are evaluated.
Invoice: Represents a billing record generated through the applicable billing system.
Credit: Represents an authorized commercial adjustment.
4. PLAN ARCHITECTURE
A Plan shall define a controlled collection of OneDraft capabilities and commercial limits.
A plan may specify:
Features
Project Limits
Storage Limits
Modeling Capacity
Drawing Generation Capacity
AI Usage
Export Capabilities
API Capacity
User Limits
Organization Limits
Support Level
Billing Interval
Plans shall be versioned so that historical subscriptions remain associated with the plan configuration applicable at the relevant time.
5. PLAN VERSIONING
Plan definitions shall be immutable once used by an active subscription except through controlled versioning.
A new plan configuration shall create a new plan version rather than silently modifying historical commercial conditions.
Historical billing and entitlement decisions shall remain reproducible using the plan version that was active when the decision occurred.
6. SUBSCRIPTION
A Subscription shall represent an organization's commercial enrollment in a OneDraft plan.
A subscription shall contain:
Subscription Identifier
Organization Identifier
Plan Identifier
Plan Version
Status
Start Date
Renewal Date
Billing Interval
Current Period
Cancellation State
Entitlement State
External Billing Reference, where applicable.
7. SUBSCRIPTION STATES
The canonical subscription states shall include:
PENDING
TRIAL
ACTIVE
PAST_DUE
SUSPENDED
CANCELLED
EXPIRED
Subscription state shall be distinct from project lifecycle state.
A project's lifecycle state shall not automatically change merely because a subscription changes state.
8. TRIAL STATE
TRIAL shall permit controlled access to specified OneDraft capabilities for a defined period or usage allowance.
Trial configuration shall specify:
Duration
Included Features
Usage Limits
Project Limits
Storage Limits
Conversion Conditions
Trial usage shall be metered according to the same authoritative usage architecture used for paid subscriptions where practical.
9. ACTIVE STATE
ACTIVE indicates that the subscription is currently entitled to the capabilities defined by its plan and applicable commercial configuration.
An active subscription shall permit entitlement evaluation according to its plan, organization and applicable usage conditions.
10. PAST_DUE STATE
PAST_DUE indicates that a billing condition requires attention while the subscription remains subject to the applicable grace and entitlement rules.
The system shall distinguish between billing delinquency and immediate loss of access.
Grace periods and restricted-access policies shall be configurable.
11. SUSPENDED STATE
SUSPENDED indicates that subscription capabilities have been restricted according to defined commercial policy.
Suspension shall not delete project information.
Existing project artifacts and historical records shall remain subject to their applicable retention and access policies.
12. CANCELLED STATE
CANCELLED indicates that the subscription has been terminated.
Cancellation shall preserve commercial history and shall not inherently delete organization or project data.
The organization may retain access to data according to the applicable plan, contractual configuration and administrative policies.
13. EXPIRED STATE
EXPIRED indicates that a subscription has reached the end of its permitted commercial period without renewal or continuation.
Expiration shall produce a deterministic entitlement state.
The platform shall distinguish expired subscription status from deleted organization or project data.
14. ENTITLEMENT MODEL
An Entitlement defines whether a specific organization or user may consume a particular OneDraft capability.
Representative entitlements include:
Project Creation
Project Modeling
Geometry Computation
Compliance Analysis
Validation
Drawing Generation
CAD Export
BIM Export
AI Interaction
API Access
Artifact Storage
Advanced Review
Administrative Controls
Entitlements shall be evaluated using authoritative subscription and organization information.
15. ENTITLEMENT TYPES
Entitlements may be:
Boolean: Capability is enabled or disabled.
Quantity-Based: Capability is limited to a defined quantity.
Rate-Based: Capability is limited within a defined time interval.
Resource-Based: Capability depends upon available computational or storage resources.
Role-Based: Capability depends upon organizational role.
Project-Based: Capability applies to specified project types or quantities.
16. ENTITLEMENT EVALUATION
An entitlement request shall be evaluated against:
Organization
Subscription
Plan
Plan Version
Feature
Usage
Quota
Project
User
Authorization
Administrative Configuration
The resulting decision shall identify whether the operation is permitted.
17. ENTITLEMENT DECISION
An entitlement decision shall contain sufficient information to explain the result.
Decision: Allowed or denied.
Entitlement: Capability evaluated.
Subject: Organization, user or project.
Reason: Deterministic reason for the decision.
Quota State: Applicable remaining or consumed quantity.
Plan: Relevant commercial configuration.
Timestamp: Time of evaluation.
Entitlement decisions may be logged for operational and audit purposes.
18. QUOTA ARCHITECTURE
Quotas shall establish controlled limits for resource consumption.
Representative quotas include:
Projects Per Organization
Users Per Organization
Storage
AI Requests
AI Compute
Drawing Generation
Geometry Computation
Exports
API Requests
Background Jobs
Quotas shall be configurable by plan and organization.
19. QUOTA PERIODS
Quota measurements may operate over:
Billing Period
Calendar Period
Daily Period
Monthly Period
Project Lifetime
Organization Lifetime
The applicable period shall be explicitly defined by the quota configuration.
20. USAGE METERING
OneDraft shall maintain an authoritative usage-metering architecture.
Usage shall be generated from measurable system events.
Potential usage dimensions include:
AI Tokens
AI Requests
Geometry Compute Time
Drawing Generation Jobs
Export Operations
Storage Consumption
API Requests
Worker Runtime
Project Count
Usage records shall identify the organization and applicable project where appropriate.
21. USAGE EVENTS
Usage shall be captured through structured events.
A usage event shall include:
Usage Event Identifier
Organization Identifier
Project Identifier, where applicable.
Meter Type
Quantity
Unit
Source Event
Timestamp
Actor
Correlation Identifier
Usage events shall be immutable after authoritative recording.
22. USAGE AGGREGATION
Usage events shall be aggregated into authoritative usage totals.
Aggregation shall support:
Current Period Usage
Historical Usage
Project Usage
Organization Usage
Meter Usage
Quota Consumption
Aggregation shall be deterministic and reproducible.
23. IDEMPOTENCY
Usage recording shall support idempotency.
The same underlying operational event shall not create duplicate billable usage merely because a worker, event consumer or API request was retried.
Usage events shall therefore possess unique identifiers or equivalent idempotency keys.
24. BILLING PERIOD
Each subscription shall have a defined billing period.
The billing period shall determine:
Usage Window
Quota Reset
Recurring Charges
Billing Events
Subscription Renewal
The system shall preserve historical billing-period records.
25. INVOICING
Where OneDraft provides or integrates with invoicing functionality, invoices shall represent commercial records associated with the organization and subscription.
An invoice may include:
Subscription Charges
Usage Charges
Credits
Adjustments
Taxes
Billing Period
Invoice Status
External payment-provider identifiers shall be stored as references rather than becoming authoritative project records.
26. PAYMENT PROVIDER INTEGRATION
OneDraft may integrate with external payment and billing providers.
External providers shall handle payment processing where applicable.
OneDraft shall maintain the minimum required commercial state needed to evaluate subscriptions and entitlements.
Payment credentials shall not be stored within the OneDraft application unless explicitly required and appropriately secured.
27. BILLING WEBHOOKS
External billing events shall be processed through controlled webhook or integration endpoints.
Incoming billing events shall be:
Authenticated
Validated
Idempotently Processed
Recorded
Mapped to Organization
Mapped to Subscription
Converted into Internal Commercial Events
External billing events shall not directly mutate arbitrary project records.
28. COMMERCIAL EVENT BUS
Commercial events shall integrate with the OneDraft event architecture.
Representative events include:
SubscriptionCreated
SubscriptionActivated
SubscriptionRenewed
SubscriptionPastDue
SubscriptionSuspended
SubscriptionCancelled
SubscriptionExpired
UsageRecorded
QuotaExceeded
EntitlementChanged
Commercial events shall be versioned and traceable.
29. FEATURE ENTITLEMENTS
Platform features shall be represented through controlled feature identifiers.
Example identifiers may include:
feature.project.create
feature.modeling
feature.compliance
feature.validation
feature.drawing.generate
feature.cad.export
feature.bim.export
feature.aria
feature.api
feature.advanced.review
Feature identifiers shall remain stable while their commercial availability is controlled through versioned configuration.
30. ARIA ENTITLEMENTS
Aria access shall be governed by explicit commercial entitlements where the selected OneDraft plan requires it.
Entitlement controls may govern:
Aria Availability
Request Volume
Model Access
Advanced Reasoning Features
Context Capacity
Generation Capacity
Automation Features
API-Based Aria Access
Commercial limits shall not alter the correctness requirements imposed by the AI architecture.
31. AI USAGE METERING
AI usage shall be metered independently from AI decision quality.
Usage metrics may include:
Requests
Input Tokens
Output Tokens
Model Runtime
Compute Units
Tool Operations
Generated Artifacts
The billing architecture shall consume authoritative usage events from the AI runtime rather than attempting to reconstruct usage from frontend activity.
32. COMPUTATIONAL USAGE
Geometry and computational operations may be metered where commercial plans require capacity controls.
Potential metrics include:
CPU Time
Worker Runtime
Geometry Operations
Model Regeneration
Rendering Operations
Large-Model Processing
Computational usage shall be measured by the execution architecture.
33. STORAGE ENTITLEMENTS
Artifact and object storage consumption may be subject to organizational quotas.
Storage calculations shall distinguish between:
Active Artifacts
Historical Artifacts
Imported Artifacts
Generated Artifacts
Temporary Processing Artifacts
Retention and deletion rules shall remain governed by the Data Lifecycle architecture.
34. API ENTITLEMENTS
API usage may be governed through plan-specific limits.
Controls may include:
Requests Per Minute
Requests Per Hour
Requests Per Billing Period
Concurrent Requests
API Feature Access
API entitlement evaluation shall integrate with the API Gateway and Rate Limiting architecture.
35. ORGANIZATION ADMINISTRATION
Organization administrators shall have access to appropriate commercial controls.
Administrative functions may include:
Plan Inspection
Subscription Inspection
Usage Monitoring
Entitlement Inspection
User Allocation
Project Allocation
Billing Configuration
Payment Configuration
Administrative access shall be controlled through the Identity, Access and Authorization architecture.
36. USAGE DASHBOARD
The frontend shall provide an organization-level usage interface.
The dashboard may display:
Current Plan
Subscription State
Billing Period
Usage
Remaining Quotas
Enabled Features
Usage Trends
Subscription Events
The displayed information shall originate from authoritative commercial services.
37. QUOTA ENFORCEMENT
Quota enforcement shall occur before the protected operation is accepted.
Where a quota is exceeded, the application shall return a deterministic entitlement or quota result.
The frontend shall communicate the condition without exposing internal implementation details.
Quota enforcement shall not rely solely upon frontend counters.
38. SOFT AND HARD LIMITS
Commercial limits may be defined as:
Soft Limit: Usage threshold that generates a warning while permitting continued operation.
Hard Limit: Usage threshold that prevents additional consumption.
The applicable limit behavior shall be defined by plan configuration.
39. OVERAGE
Plans may support controlled overage policies.
Where overage is enabled, the system shall record consumption beyond the included quota and associate the excess usage with the applicable billing configuration.
Where overage is disabled, the applicable operation shall be denied once the hard limit is reached.
40. CREDITS AND ADJUSTMENTS
Authorized commercial credits and adjustments shall be represented as explicit records.
Each adjustment shall identify:
Adjustment Identifier
Organization
Subscription
Amount or Quantity
Reason
Authorized Actor
Timestamp
Source
Credits shall not modify historical usage events.
41. COMMERCIAL AUDIT
Commercial operations shall be auditable.
The system shall retain records of:
Subscription Changes
Plan Changes
Entitlement Changes
Usage
Quota Changes
Credits
Billing Events
Administrative Actions
Historical commercial state shall remain reconstructable.
42. PLAN CHANGES
An organization may change plans through an authorized commercial operation.
Plan changes shall create explicit subscription records and effective dates.
The system shall define whether the change is:
Immediate
End-of-Period
Scheduled
The entitlement service shall evaluate the appropriate plan according to the effective timestamp.
43. DOWNGRADE PROTECTION
A plan downgrade shall not silently destroy project information.
Where a downgrade causes the organization to exceed newly applicable quotas, the system shall establish a controlled restricted state.
Existing project data shall remain subject to applicable access and retention policies.
The system shall clearly identify conditions that must be resolved.
44. UPGRADE
An upgrade shall create the applicable entitlement state according to the effective subscription configuration.
New capabilities shall become available only after the authoritative subscription state confirms the upgrade.
Frontend presentation shall follow the authoritative entitlement state.
45. CANCELLATION PROTECTION
Subscription cancellation shall preserve historical billing and project records.
The system shall clearly distinguish:
Subscription Cancellation
from
Organization Deletion
and
Project Deletion
These are separate operations governed by separate architectures.
46. DATA MODEL
The billing architecture shall support structured commercial entities.
Representative entities include:
Plan
PlanVersion
Subscription
SubscriptionPeriod
Entitlement
Quota
Meter
UsageEvent
UsageAggregate
Invoice
BillingEvent
Credit
CommercialAdjustment
EntitlementDecision
BillingConfiguration
These structures shall integrate with Organization, User, Project and API usage structures without becoming replacements for those domain entities.
47. API ARCHITECTURE
The billing API shall expose controlled commercial resources.
Representative endpoints include:
GET /organizations/{id}/billing
GET /organizations/{id}/subscription
GET /organizations/{id}/entitlements
GET /organizations/{id}/usage
GET /organizations/{id}/quotas
GET /organizations/{id}/billing/history
POST /organizations/{id}/subscription
POST /organizations/{id}/subscription/change
POST /organizations/{id}/subscription/cancel
POST /billing/webhooks
The final API contract shall remain governed by the established OneDraft OpenAPI architecture.
48. SECURITY, TESTING & OPERATIONAL INTEGRITY
Billing and entitlement services shall be protected by the OneDraft identity and authorization architecture.
Testing shall include:
Plan Evaluation
Subscription Transitions
Entitlement Decisions
Quota Enforcement
Usage Metering
Idempotency
Billing Webhooks
Plan Changes
Cancellation
Upgrade
Downgrade
Concurrent Usage
Failure Recovery
Security
Commercial decisions shall be deterministic and observable.
The billing system shall not become a privileged pathway for bypassing project authorization, lifecycle controls or security boundaries.
49. ARCHITECTURAL INVARIANTS
The following invariants shall govern the Billing, Subscription & Entitlement Architecture.
Commercial Separation: Billing state shall remain separate from authoritative project state.
Deterministic Entitlements: Identical commercial state and entitlement inputs shall produce the same entitlement result.
Backend Enforcement: Commercial limits shall not depend solely upon frontend controls.
Historical Integrity: Historical subscription and usage records shall remain preserved.
Idempotent Metering: A single operational event shall not be counted more than once.
No Project Destruction: Commercial state changes shall not silently destroy project information.
Explicit Plan Changes: Upgrades and downgrades shall produce explicit commercial records.
Controlled Access: Entitlement decisions shall remain subject to identity and authorization.
AI Separation: AI usage metering shall not determine AI correctness or validation.
Tenant Association: Commercial records shall remain associated with the correct organization.
Auditability: Commercial state changes shall remain traceable.
Provider Independence: External payment providers shall not become authoritative sources for OneDraft project state.
50. COMPLETION STATUS
The Billing, Subscription & Entitlement Architecture establishes the complete commercial-control layer for OneDraft.
The subsystem defines plans, plan versions, subscriptions, subscription states, entitlements, quotas, usage metering, AI usage, computational usage, storage limits, API limits, billing periods, invoicing, external payment integration, commercial events, upgrades, downgrades, cancellation, credits, overage and commercial auditing.
The architecture establishes a controlled relationship between the OneDraft commercial model and the capabilities available to organizations while preserving the independence and integrity of project data, Project Model state, lifecycle state, validation, documentation and security.
The next architectural layer establishes the formal Multi-Tenant Organization & Project Isolation architecture, defining how organizations, users, projects, resources, quotas and data remain isolated within the OneDraft platform.
